                <pb facs="0527" n="517" sameAs="frus:frus1941v06/pg517"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">832.796/300: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Brazil</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Caffery</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Rio de
                                Janeiro</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1941-04-09T19:00:00-03:00">April 9, 1941—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 8:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>291. For the Under Secretary. Your 178 of April 7, 8 p.m. Aranha says that he has
                    no idea of making difficulties for our oil companies on this but that he is
                    extremely reluctant to do anything to interrupt the Lati service: it is he says
                    the only effective means aside from cable that he has of communicating with many
                    of his representatives in Europe and North and West Africa.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Caffery</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
